"""Predeclared texts and controlled exact-token interventions."""
TOPICS=[
('rain','Explain how rain forms, following water from the ocean to a cloud and back to the ground.',
'Sunlight warms the surface of the ocean and supplies energy that allows some liquid water to become water vapor. The vapor mixes with the air above the surface. Moving air carries this moisture away, and rising air transports it to cooler heights. As the air cools, water vapor condenses onto tiny particles suspended in the atmosphere. Many small droplets collect together to form a cloud. Collisions between droplets allow some of them to grow. When the drops become large enough, gravity carries them downward through the cloud. Some water evaporates again before reaching the surface, especially when the air below the cloud is dry. The remaining drops reach the ground as rain. Water then flows into streams or sinks into the soil. Rivers eventually carry much of it back to the ocean. This movement of water between the surface and the atmosphere is called the water cycle.'),
('bicycle','Explain how a bicycle moves and stops, including the rider, chain, wheels and brakes.',
'A rider pushes on the pedals and turns the crank attached to the front chainring. The chain transfers that motion to a sprocket connected to the rear wheel. As the rear wheel rotates against the ground, the bicycle moves forward. The rider balances by adjusting the handlebars and shifting body weight slightly. Different gears change the relationship between pedal speed and wheel speed. A lower gear makes climbing easier because each turn of the pedals moves the bicycle a shorter distance. A higher gear is useful on level ground when the bicycle is already moving quickly. To slow down, the rider squeezes a brake lever. The brake presses pads against a rotating surface and friction converts motion into heat. Both tires need enough grip to transfer braking forces to the road. Wet or loose surfaces reduce that grip. For a controlled stop, the rider applies the brakes smoothly and watches the road ahead.'),
('bread','Explain the main stages of making a simple loaf of bread.',
'The baker begins by measuring flour, water, yeast and salt into a bowl. Mixing brings the ingredients together and starts to hydrate the flour. Kneading develops a network of gluten that can hold gas inside the dough. The dough then rests in a covered container so that its surface does not dry out. During this first rise, yeast consumes available sugars and releases carbon dioxide. Small pockets of gas expand within the dough and make it lighter. The baker gently shapes the risen dough into a loaf and places it in a tin or on a tray. A second rise gives the shaped loaf time to expand again. Meanwhile, the oven heats to the required temperature. In the oven, the trapped gas expands further and the loaf grows before its structure sets. Moisture leaves the surface and a crust begins to form. After baking, the loaf cools on a rack so that steam can escape before it is sliced.'),
('library','Describe a calm afternoon in a public library as a short story.',
'The afternoon light reached across the reading tables and settled on a row of empty chairs. Mara carried a notebook to a seat near the window. She had come to find a book about the history of the town, but for a moment she simply listened. A page turned somewhere behind her. At the front desk, a librarian spoke quietly to a visitor and pointed toward the shelves. Mara opened the catalog and wrote down a number. She walked along a narrow aisle until she found a blue volume with a faded title. Inside were photographs of streets she recognized, although the buildings looked smaller and the roads were almost empty. She brought the book back to the table and began making notes. Outside, a bus stopped at the corner and a few people hurried past the window. Inside, the clock moved toward closing time without disturbing the stillness of the room.'),
('seasons','Explain why Earth has seasons without suggesting its distance from the Sun is the main cause.',
'Earth travels around the Sun while its axis remains tilted relative to the plane of its orbit. This tilt changes how directly sunlight reaches different parts of the planet over the year. When the northern hemisphere tilts toward the Sun, its days are longer and sunlight arrives at a steeper angle. The same amount of incoming energy is concentrated over a smaller surface area. These conditions tend to warm the ground and produce summer in the north. At the same time, the southern hemisphere receives shorter days and less direct sunlight. Six months later, the situation is reversed. Near the equator, changes in day length are smaller than they are near the poles. The atmosphere and oceans also store and transport heat, so the warmest days do not always occur at the exact time of the longest daylight. Seasonal weather therefore depends on both the geometry of sunlight and the way heat moves through the climate system.'),
('summary','Summarize a community garden project: volunteers repaired beds, installed a water tank, planted vegetables and scheduled weekly maintenance.',
'The community garden project brought together residents who wanted to restore an unused growing space. Volunteers first checked the condition of the raised beds and replaced damaged boards. They removed weeds, added fresh compost and marked paths between the planting areas. A water tank was installed to provide a nearby supply during dry weather. The group then selected vegetables that could grow well in the local climate and planted them in the prepared beds. New volunteers received a short explanation of watering and basic care. To keep the work manageable, the organizers created a weekly maintenance schedule. Different teams would check soil moisture, remove weeds and look for signs of pests. The project combined physical repairs with a plan for continuing care. Its success would depend on keeping that routine through the growing season and making sure that responsibilities remained clear as new people joined the group.'),
('tea','Explain how to make tea for several guests, with practical steps.',
'Begin by checking how many guests would like tea and whether anyone prefers a particular variety. Fill the kettle with enough fresh water for the number of cups you plan to serve. While the water heats, place clean cups on a tray and prepare the tea leaves or bags. Different teas benefit from different water temperatures, so follow the instructions on the packet when they are available. Pour the hot water over the tea and allow it to steep for the recommended time. Removing the leaves or bags at the right point helps prevent the drink from becoming too bitter. Set out milk, lemon or sugar separately so that each guest can choose what to add. Carry the tray carefully and leave space around each cup. If people arrive at different times, it may be better to prepare a second fresh pot rather than leave the first one standing for too long.'),
('bridge','Describe how engineers plan a small pedestrian bridge.',
'Engineers begin by understanding where people need to cross and what conditions the bridge must withstand. They survey the ground on both sides and measure the distance between suitable supports. They also consider water levels, local weather and the needs of people using wheelchairs or bicycles. The design must carry the expected weight of pedestrians while resisting wind and other forces. Engineers compare materials and structural forms before selecting an approach that fits the site and budget. Foundations transfer loads safely into the ground, so soil conditions require careful attention. Drawings specify dimensions, connections and protective surfaces. Before construction starts, the plans are reviewed for safety and compliance with local requirements. During building work, inspections confirm that the materials and connections match the design. Once the bridge opens, regular maintenance helps identify wear, corrosion or damage before it becomes a serious problem.'),
('compost','Explain how a compost pile turns garden waste into useful material.',
'A compost pile contains plant material that small organisms can break down. A useful mixture includes moist green material, such as fresh clippings, and drier brown material, such as fallen leaves. The organisms need water and air as well as food. If the pile becomes too dry, decomposition slows. If it becomes waterlogged, air cannot move through it easily and unpleasant odors may develop. Turning the pile mixes the materials and introduces oxygen. As microorganisms work, they release heat, and the center of a large active pile may become noticeably warm. Over time, recognizable leaves and stems become darker and smaller. The finished material has a crumbly texture and an earthy smell. Gardeners can add it to soil to improve structure and help retain moisture. Composting also keeps useful organic material out of general waste and returns some nutrients to the garden.'),
('maps','Explain how to read a simple walking map.',
'Start by finding the map legend, which explains the symbols used for paths, roads and landmarks. Check the scale to understand how distance on the page relates to distance on the ground. A north arrow shows the direction in which the map is oriented. Locate your current position using a clearly identifiable feature, such as a station or a road junction. Then trace the route to your destination and note the turns you will need to make. It helps to divide a longer walk into short sections between recognizable landmarks. Contour lines indicate changes in height, with closely spaced lines usually showing a steeper slope. Before setting off, consider whether the route crosses busy roads or includes steps. During the walk, compare the map with what you can see around you. If the features stop matching, pause and confirm your position rather than continuing on an uncertain path.'),
('sleep','Explain how an ordinary evening routine can support consistent sleep.',
'A consistent evening routine gives the body a predictable transition from daytime activity to rest. Begin by choosing a bedtime that allows enough time for sleep before the morning alarm. In the hour before bed, reduce demanding work and select a quieter activity. Preparing clothes and other essentials for the next day can make it easier to stop thinking about unfinished tasks. A comfortable room is usually dark, quiet and neither too hot nor too cold. Bright screens and stimulating conversations may make it harder to settle, so some people find it useful to put devices aside. The same routine will not suit everyone, and occasional disrupted nights are common. What matters is establishing habits that can be repeated without much difficulty. If sleep problems continue or have a serious effect on daily life, a qualified health professional can help assess the situation.'),
('train','Write a short narrative about waiting for a train on an ordinary morning.',
'Jon reached the platform with several minutes to spare and checked the departure board. The train was on time. He stood beside a bench and watched a small group of passengers gather near the yellow line. Someone folded a newspaper while another person adjusted the strap of a heavy bag. A recorded announcement named the next station, and a light breeze moved through the open end of the platform. Jon took a book from his coat pocket, but before he found his place he heard the train approaching. The rails began to hum and the headlights appeared around the curve. People stepped back as the first carriage passed. When the doors opened, he waited for the arriving passengers to leave before getting on. He found a seat by the window and put his bag at his feet. Outside, the station clock moved forward as the train began to pull away.')]
